  <paragraphs xml:id="description">
    <title>Course Description</title>
    <p>
      In this course we will study the following:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            the fundamentals of probability including discrete and continuous random variables, expected value, variance, and conditional probability,
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            data exploration, manipulation, and visualization, and
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            the fundamentals of hypothesis testing.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      All topics will be studied using modern computational software. 
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="instructional-format">
    <title>Instructional Format</title>
    <p>This is a face-to-face course, which means we will meet in person.</p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="course-materials">
    <title>Course Materials</title>
    <p>
      Required Text: <term>Probability, Statistics, and Data: A Fresh Approach Using R</term>, by Darrin Speegle. ISBN: 9780367436674. <em>This book is available freely online at</em> <url href="https://probstatsdata.com/"> https://probstatsdata.com/ </url>
    </p>
    <p>
      Additionally, you will be required to use a computer with RStudio installed and have an internet connection for homework.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="office-hours">
    <title>Student Office Hours</title>
    <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Erich McAlister's Office Hours:</em> MWF 3:30-4:30pm in the PEC.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="feedback">
    <title>Feedback and Communication</title>
    <p>
      Communication of all course information will occur via Canvas as well as in class. Most assignments will be submitted and graded online in Canvas or Webwork.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="missing-class">
    <!--Course policies are made by intructional teams; these are examples.-->
    <title>Missing Class</title>
    <p>
            It is expected that you will be in class, on time, for every class meeting. This maximizes your opportunity to learn and your teacher's ability to help you learn. If you must miss class, be sure to check Canvas and Webwork for assignments, and try to get notes from a classmate. If you're still having difficulty with the material, then be sure to use office hours to get caught up. If you need an extension on an assignment, just ask your professor and it will likely be granted.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you need to miss class for an extended period of time, let your professor know so that we may work out a remote accommodation.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="tech">
    <title>Technology and Software Requirements</title>
    <p>
      You will need to be able to access Canvas to submit assignments online. Internet access and a scanning device are required. Exams will be proctored in a computer lab with necessary software (RStudio) installed on all lab computers.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
</section>
